Plan: dalogin login/logout error handling and session-state integrity

Feature: `speckit.system.dalogin-login-logout-error-handling.specify`

Intent
Forward-only. This pass changes behaviour deliberately: failures that are currently invisible become
visible. There is no rollback plan; defects found afterwards are fixed forward.

Sequencing
- Depends on `dalogin-datasource-legacy-removal` for the narrowing of `throws Exception` to
  `throws SQLException`, which makes "which failures are possible" answerable.
- Should follow `dalogin-sqlaccess-cdi-injection`, so error handling is written once against
  injected beans rather than twice.
- The combined persistence/Servlet adaptation now implements the login-side requirements
  (`FR-001`-`FR-007`) together with the final `DeviceSessionManager` boundary.
- Run this remaining feature afterward for logout ownership, listener cleanup, and schema work.

Design decisions
- Signal failure by exception, not by `boolean`. A `boolean` is the reason `B-002` exists: it is
  discardable at the call site and invisible in review. A dedicated unchecked exception carrying the
  failed operation and device identifier satisfies `FR-004` and `FR-005` without forcing every
  caller into a `try`/`catch` cascade.
- Write to the database before publishing the session. Today attributes are set first, which is what
  makes `activeUsers` diverge (`B-004`). Reordering so the writes precede
  `session.setAttribute("user"/"deviceId", ...)` satisfies `FR-002`. The listener remains untouched:
  it keeps reacting to `attributeAdded`, it just fires later.
- Where a session already exists when a write fails, invalidate it (`FR-003`). That path also fires
  `sessionDestroyed`, so the cleanup logic must tolerate a session that never reached the database —
  this is exactly the `B-005` case and must not log as an error.
- Give logout a single owner (`FR-009`). `sessionDestroyed` fires for both explicit logout and
  timeout, so it is the correct single place to call the procedure; `LoggingOut` should invalidate
  and let the listener do the database work. This removes the double call without losing the timeout
  path.
- Distinguish zero-rows from success by returning the affected-row count rather than a `boolean`
  (`FR-008`). Zero rows is legitimate on a timeout of a login that never completed, so it is a WARN,
  not an error.
- Treat the `Tokens` triggers as load-bearing and leave the mechanism alone. Rotation is the
  intended revocation path (owner-confirmed): validation compares a client value against the stored
  `token2`, so regenerating it invalidates the old one. The schema work is therefore narrow —
  document the mechanism (`FR-014`) and resolve one malformed statement (`FR-012`) — not a
  redesign. Anything that disables `Tokens_BEFORE_UPDATE` is out of bounds.

Implementation phases
1. Complete the remaining failure signal
   - Reuse the persistence exception introduced by the combined adaptation.
   - Convert `logout` to report failure through it.
   - Return the affected-row count from the logout path.
2. Verify absorbed login work
   - Confirm `establishLogin` is atomic, pending sessions are invalidated on failure, attributes are
     published after commit, and the token retry is absent.
3. Logout call-site correction
   - Make `sessionDestroyed` the single caller of the logout procedure.
   - Reduce `LoggingOut` to invalidation.
   - Raise the swallowed DEBUG catch to WARN with identifiers, and keep remaining cleanup running.
4. Schema documentation and cleanup
   - Document the rotation mechanism in the schema source, including why the `Last_seen_AFTER_UPDATE`
     self-assignment must not be "optimised away".
   - Resolve the malformed `logout_device` assignment without changing rotation behaviour.
   - Verify rotation still occurs after the change.
5. Verification
   - Static review against the acceptance criteria.
   - Run `LoginFlowTest` to confirm the success path is unchanged.
   - Exercise the failure path with the database stopped.

Validation strategy
- The existing `LoginFlowTest` (`loginSuccess`, `loginBadHmac`, `loginWrongPassword`) is the
  success-path gate. It must pass unchanged; a behavioural change that breaks it is a defect in this
  feature, not an expected consequence.
- The failure path needs a test that does not exist yet: a login attempted with the database
  unreachable, asserting 5xx and an empty `/activeSessions`. This is the only new test this feature
  requires.
- `AC-007` and `AC-011` both assert rotation still works. Since the schema change is deliberately
  narrow, a before/after comparison of `token1`/`token2` across a logout is sufficient evidence.
- Everything else in the manual block stays with the owner.

Risks and mitigations
- Risk: making failures visible surfaces pre-existing breakage as new incidents.
  - Mitigation: expected and intended. Roll out behind the owner's manual verification rather than
    silently.
- Risk: reordering session publication changes listener timing in ways the eviction logic depends on.
  - Mitigation: `attributeAdded` ordering between `user` and `deviceId` is preserved; only the
    position relative to the database writes moves. Verify device eviction still works on re-login.
- Risk: moving the logout call into the listener alone loses it if the listener does not fire.
  - Mitigation: confirm `sessionDestroyed` fires on explicit `invalidate()` before removing the call
    from `LoggingOut`. It does today — that is the source of the double call.
- Risk: a cleanup of the `logout_device` statement accidentally suppresses rotation.
  - Mitigation: rotation comes from the trigger, not the statement, so any `UPDATE` to the row
    preserves it. Removing the `UPDATE` entirely would not. Verify with `AC-011`.
- Risk: a later reader removes the `Last_seen_AFTER_UPDATE` self-assignment as dead code and
  silently disables rotation on re-login.
  - Mitigation: this is the main reason `FR-014` exists. The comment is the mitigation.
- Risk: scope creep into the token-generation weakness (`B-012`) or the `last_insert_id()` tautology
  (`B-013`).
  - Mitigation: both are explicitly out of scope and recorded for separate features.
